[2026-10-25T23:33:30Z] Matt Parrett: Proposal, after reading docs/LLVM-TARGET-SPEC.md Milestone 2 beside this issue's D197 design: build the collector once.
 
 Both milestones describe the same collector: non-moving mark-sweep in let-go over raw-memory intrinsics (word and byte loads and stores, address arithmetic, page allocation), precise tracing by kind from a layout table the emitter generates, size-segregated pages, a growth-based trigger. They differ in one place, how roots are found: the llvm spec takes them at function entry (decision 7, CPS), this one keeps a shadow stack. Everything else is one implementation with two consumers, and linear memory is raw memory.
 
 What this issue would become: the linear target is the second harness of `rt/native/gc.lg` (or wherever the llvm lane puts it), not a collector of its own. Its contribution is the shadow-stack root variant and a place to run the collector under node and wazero, where a heap can be dumped and diffed from JavaScript and a failing program re-run in seconds; the native binary gets the same code with its own root discovery. The two consumers keep each other honest the way the two targets already do on the oracle rows.
 
 What it asks of the llvm lane: that M2's collector be written against the arch layer 646deae names, with root discovery behind one function, and that the layout table come from the emitter in a form both targets can emit. Nothing about its order of work changes; this issue stays deferred until that collector exists, then takes the shadow-stack variant and the wazero rows.
 
 Facts that would close this issue, under the proposal: F1 the linear module runs `corpus/wasm` under the wazero runner with the shared collector and the shadow stack, matching native on the rows the GC lane matches; F2 one collector source, two root-discovery implementations, no second mark or sweep. If the llvm lane would rather keep M2 native-only, say so here and this issue keeps D197 as written.
